  "untried_combinations": [
    {
      "idea_sketch": "BEq-guided expert iteration for autoformalization: Use BEq as the reward signal (instead of simple compilation checking) in a Lean-STaR-style expert iteration loop for autoformalization models. Generate k candidate formalizations, score them with BEq against each other (no ground truth needed—just mutual consistency), retain the BEq-consistent cluster, and fine-tune. This replaces the weak 'does it compile?' signal with a semantically-aware 'is it equivalent to what most candidates converge on?' signal.",
      "from_papers": ["BEq/RAutoformalizer", "Lean-STaR", "Autoformalize by Symbolic Equivalence"],
      "why_promising": "BEq provides a formally-grounded equivalence signal that is strictly more informative than compilation checking (necessary but not sufficient) or embedding similarity (approximate). Lean-STaR proved expert iteration works with binary Lean verification; upgrading the reward signal to BEq-level semantic equivalence should yield higher-quality training data. The Symbolic Equivalence paper already showed that consensus voting among candidates works—BEq would formalize this intuition."
    },
    {
      "idea_sketch": "Dependency-aware TTRL: Combine RAutoformalizer's dependency retrieval with AlphaProof's Test-Time Reinforcement Learning. At inference time, retrieve relevant Mathlib dependencies for the target problem, generate formal variants using dependency-conditioned autoformalization, and run TTRL on this retrieval-augmented synthetic curriculum. This addresses AlphaProof's autoformalization bottleneck while making TTRL more sample-efficient through better problem-specific context.",
      "from_papers": ["AlphaProof", "BEq/RAutoformalizer", "Lean Workbook"],
      "why_promising": "AlphaProof's TTRL adds +15 percentage points but wastes compute generating variants that may not leverage relevant library infrastructure. RAutoformalizer's oracle retrieval experiment shows 3.3× improvement with perfect dependencies. Combining them means TTRL variants would be grounded in actual Mathlib content, producing more useful training signal per compute dollar. This directly attacks two of the field's biggest gaps simultaneously: autoformalization quality and compute efficiency."
    },
    {
      "idea_sketch": "Thought-augmented autoformalization: Apply Lean-STaR's retrospective thought synthesis not to tactic generation but to the autoformalization step itself. For each (informal statement, formal statement) pair, use an LLM to generate a natural-language 'translation rationale' explaining the mapping decisions (why this Lean type, why this Mathlib definition, what semantic nuance is preserved). Then train an autoformalization model that generates thought→formal-statement sequences, using BEq to filter correct translations during expert iteration.",
      "from_papers": ["Lean-STaR", "BEq/RAutoformalizer", "MUSTARD"],
      "why_promising": "Lean-STaR showed that interleaving informal thoughts with formal outputs improves formal proving by 2.9 percentage points. Autoformalization is arguably a harder translation task where the reasoning-before-acting benefit should be even larger. BEq provides the verification signal that Lean compilation provides for proofs. This creates a thinking autoformalization model that can explain its translation decisions, enabling better debugging and human-AI collaboration."
    },
    {
      "idea_sketch": "Curriculum-aware large-scale data synthesis: Combine MUSTARD's concept-seeded generation with Lean Workbook's active-learning correction loop and AlphaProof's matchmaker curriculum system. Generate problems at controlled difficulty levels using structured concept sampling, validate with Lean compilation + BEq equivalence checking, diagnose systematic errors via active learning, and organize verified data into a curriculum ordered by difficulty and domain. Feed this curriculum to an RL-based prover with interestingness-based problem routing.",
      "from_papers": ["MUSTARD", "Lean Workbook", "AlphaProof", "BEq/RAutoformalizer"],
      "why_promising": "Each paper solves one piece: MUSTARD handles diverse generation, Lean Workbook handles scale + correction, AlphaProof handles curriculum design, BEq handles faithful evaluation. No paper combines all four into a single end-to-end pipeline. The curriculum dimension is especially underexplored—current datasets are unstructured collections, but RL provers benefit enormously from difficulty-matched training (AlphaProof's matchmaker was critical to its success)."
    },
    {
      "idea_sketch": "Cross-proof-assistant autoformalization via universal intermediate representation: Train a model to translate informal math to an abstract intermediate formal representation, then compile to multiple targets (Lean 4, Coq, Isabelle). Use BEq-style equivalence checking independently in each target system, and take the consensus across proof assistants as a stronger verification signal than any single system provides.",
      "from_papers": ["BEq/RAutoformalizer", "Autoformalize by Symbolic Equivalence", "Lean Workbook"],
      "why_promising": "All current work is locked to a single proof assistant (usually Lean 4). A universal intermediate representation would unlock training data from all formal libraries simultaneously (Mathlib + Mathematical Components + AFP), dramatically expanding the data landscape. The multi-system consensus idea extends the Symbolic Equivalence paper's voting approach from multiple candidates in one system to single candidates verified across systems—a strictly stronger correctness signal."
    },
    {
      "idea_sketch": "Formal verification-guided informalization for mathematical education: Invert the pipeline—start from Mathlib proofs, use BEq's topological informalization to generate natural-language explanations, then use Lean-STaR-style thought augmentation to interleave pedagogical commentary with formal steps. Evaluate via human studies on mathematical comprehension rather than standard NLP metrics.",
      "from_papers": ["BEq/RAutoformalizer (topological informalization)", "Lean-STaR", "AlphaProof (bidirectional autoformalization question)"],
      "why_promising": "Every paper in this area goes informal→formal. The reverse direction (formal→informal) is equally valuable but almost completely unexplored as a primary research target. BEq's topological informalization and AlphaProof's open question about bidirectional translation both point here. This opens a new application domain (education/explanation) that could attract a different reviewer pool (HCI, education) while using the same technical infrastructure."
    }
  ],
  "method_building_blocks": [
    {
      "tool": "Lean 4 type checker as binary RL reward",
      "what_it_does": "Provides an incorruptible, zero-cost, binary (correct/incorrect) reward signal by compiling candidate proofs against Lean's kernel. Transforms subjective quality assessment into objective verification. Can process thousands of candidates in parallel when compiled to C (6× speedup).",
      "used_by": ["AlphaProof", "Lean-STaR", "MUSTARD", "Lean Workbook"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Code verification (type systems in Rust/Haskell as reward signals for code generation RL), chemical synthesis verification (retrosynthesis planners verified against reaction databases), logical reasoning tasks where SAT/SMT solvers can verify candidate solutions"
    },
    {
      "tool": "Retrospective rationale synthesis via oracle LLM",
      "what_it_does": "Given a ground-truth (input, output) pair where the reasoning trace is missing, asks a frontier LLM to generate a plausible natural-language rationale that explains the output, creating synthetic (input, rationale, output) triples for training. Quality is bootstrapped via expert iteration against a verifier.",
      "used_by": ["Lean-STaR"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Any domain where expert actions are recorded without explanations: surgical procedure logs (actions without reasoning), chess game databases (moves without strategic commentary), software commit histories (diffs without design rationales). Also applicable to autoformalization: generating 'translation rationales' for (informal, formal) pairs."
    },
    {
      "tool": "BEq (Bidirectional Extended Definitional Equivalence)",
      "what_it_does": "Checks semantic equivalence of two formal mathematical statements by using an LLM to search for tactic-based transformation proofs (from a restricted primitive set), then verifying transformations in Lean 4's kernel. Provides a formally-grounded metric with proven reflexivity, symmetry, and transitivity, validated at 90.5% accuracy / 100% precision against expert human judgments.",
      "used_by": ["BEq/RAutoformalizer"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Reward signal for autoformalization RL (replace binary compilation with semantic equivalence), deduplication of large formal datasets (Lean Workbook's near-duplicate problem), automated grading of student formalizations, quality filtering in any generate-then-verify pipeline"
    },
    {
      "tool": "Topological informalization of formal libraries",
      "what_it_does": "Parses the full dependency graph of a formal library (e.g., 243,797 objects in Mathlib 4), orders objects by dependency depth (24 generations), and informalizes bottom-up so that each object's informalization can reference already-informalized dependencies. Produces a complete informal mirror of the formal library.",
      "used_by": ["BEq/RAutoformalizer"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Creating training data for retrieval-augmented autoformalization at library scale, generating natural-language documentation for formal libraries, building concept taxonomies for curriculum design in formal math training, enabling search over formal libraries using natural-language queries"
    },
    {
      "tool": "Active learning with formal verification + human error-pattern diagnosis",
      "what_it_does": "Iterates between: (1) running LLM autoformalization at scale, (2) filtering via compilation, (3) having human experts review a small sample of failures to identify systematic error patterns (e.g., type confusion), (4) adding ~30 corrected examples per round to training data, (5) re-fine-tuning. Achieves 93.5% accuracy with only 341 total human annotations across 6 rounds.",
      "used_by": ["Lean Workbook"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Any domain where automated verification is cheap but expert annotation is expensive: code translation (compile-check + human review of type errors), medical record structuring (schema validation + clinician review), legal document formalization (logical consistency checking + lawyer review)"
    },
    {
      "tool": "Symbolic equivalence via ATPs + semantic consistency via back-translation embeddings",
      "what_it_does": "Dual scoring system: (1) uses automated theorem provers (Sledgehammer, Z3, CVC5) to check logical equivalence between candidate formalizations after variable alignment via bipartite matching, and (2) back-translates each candidate to natural language and measures embedding similarity to the original. Log-linear combination with robust hyperparameter range (α = 0.32–0.6).",
      "used_by": ["Autoformalize by Symbolic Equivalence"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Ranking/filtering in any multi-candidate generation pipeline: code generation (test-based equivalence + docstring similarity), translation (back-translation consistency + semantic embedding), knowledge base population (logical consistency + textual faithfulness)"
    },
    {
      "tool": "AlphaZero MCTS with AND-node decomposition for multi-goal proof states",
      "what_it_does": "Adapts Monte Carlo Tree Search with PUCT selection for theorem proving, where proof states can decompose into multiple sub-goals (AND-nodes). Uses minimum-return aggregation across sub-goals and progressive sampling for dynamic action-set expansion. Paired with a dual-headed transformer (policy + value).",
      "used_by": ["AlphaProof"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Any planning domain with hierarchical goal decomposition: program synthesis (functions with multiple subroutines), multi-step chemical synthesis, project planning with dependencies, game playing in games with simultaneous sub-objectives"
    },
    {
      "tool": "Matchmaker curriculum system with interestingness scoring",
      "what_it_does": "Routes training problems to RL agents based on difficulty estimation and 'interestingness'—problems where the model is near the boundary of its capability receive priority, preventing wasted compute on trivially easy or impossibly hard problems.",
      "used_by": ["AlphaProof"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Any RL training pipeline with heterogeneous problem difficulties: adaptive tutoring systems, curriculum learning for code generation, difficulty-matched training for theorem proving at smaller scales, automated benchmark construction"
    },
    {
      "tool": "Error-message-driven iterative self-correction loop",
      "what_it_does": "When a Lean proof fails compilation, feeds the specific error messages (with line numbers) back to the LLM for targeted correction across multiple rounds. Role-separated prompting (different personas for informal vs. formal generation) maximizes diversity in initial generation.",
      "used_by": ["MUSTARD"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Code debugging pipelines (compiler error → LLM fix → recompile), configuration validation (schema errors → LLM fix → revalidate), any generate-then-verify pipeline where the verifier produces structured error diagnostics"
    },
    {
      "tool": "Dense retrieval with hard negative mining for formal dependency retrieval",
      "what_it_does": "Fine-tunes BGE-M3 embeddings with hard negatives (mined from top-100 BM25 candidates) to retrieve relevant formal library dependencies given an informal mathematical statement. Top-k retrieved dependencies are provided as context to the autoformalization model.",
      "used_by": ["BEq/RAutoformalizer"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "API-aware code generation (retrieve relevant API signatures), legal reasoning (retrieve relevant statutes/precedents), scientific formalization (retrieve relevant ontology terms), any grounded generation task where output must reference existing structured knowledge"
    }
  ],
  "common_pitfalls": [
    "Using unfaithful evaluation metrics and optimizing against them: BEq demonstrated that BLEU, simple type-checking, and LLM-based grading are all fundamentally unreliable for autoformalization evaluation. Papers that report only BLEU or compilation-pass-rate risk optimizing for a proxy that diverges from actual semantic correctness. Always include at least one formally-grounded evaluation signal, and if using a new metric, validate it against expert human judgment on a held-out sample.",
    "Treating autoformalization as pure sequence-to-sequence translation: BEq/RAutoformalizer showed that context-blind translation hallucinations formal identifiers. Lean Workbook's active learning revealed systematic type confusions (sqrt vs. Real.sqrt). Any autoformalization system that doesn't condition on the target formal library's actual contents will hit a ceiling imposed by identifier hallucination, regardless of model scale.",
    "Underinvesting in ablation studies and rebuttal preparation: MUSTARD's acceptance was decided during rebuttal when authors provided expanded human evaluation (88→200 samples), 70B model experiments, and contamination analysis. The BEq paper survived skepticism by demonstrating 7B model viability. Papers in this area should budget compute and time for at least 2-3 supplementary experiments that address predictable reviewer concerns (contamination, scalability, ablation of each component).",
    "Ignoring the pass@1 vs pass@k gap: The Symbolic Equivalence paper showed GPT-4 jumps from ~37.5% pass@1 to ~53.5% pass@10, meaning correct formalizations already exist in the candidate pool but are not selected. Papers that report only pass@1 and try to improve generation quality may be attacking the wrong bottleneck—the real opportunity is in better verification and ranking of existing candidates.",
    "Claiming 'chain-of-thought helps' without domain-specific evidence: Lean-STaR showed that thought augmentation helps only ~3% in formal proving, much less than the ~10-20% gains in mathematical word problems. The relationship between informal reasoning and formal proving is more complex than assumed. Don't naively transfer techniques from informal math reasoning and expect similar gains—the structured, per-step nature of formal proving may already capture much of what chain-of-thought provides.",
    "Overlooking data contamination in frontier-LLM-dependent pipelines: Lean-STaR reviewers raised GPT-4 contamination concerns. Any paper using GPT-4/Claude/Gemini for data generation or evaluation must proactively analyze contamination (exact match analysis, embedding-based overlap detection, held-out OOD benchmarks like Con-NF). This concern will only intensify as frontier models train on more formal math data.",
    "Underreporting compute costs and claiming 'accessibility': AlphaProof's intellectual honesty about 280,000+ TPU-days was praised, not penalized. Papers that are vague about compute requirements face reviewer suspicion. Report GPU-hours, wall-clock time, and cost estimates explicitly—reviewers in this community respect transparency and will question papers that seem computationally unreproducible.",
    "Over-relying on miniF2F as the sole evaluation benchmark: Multiple papers use miniF2F as the primary evaluation (Lean-STaR, Autoformalize by Symbolic Equivalence). While it's the standard benchmark, it contains only 488 problems at the competition level. Papers that only evaluate on miniF2F face questions about generalization. Include at least one additional benchmark (PutnamBench, formal-imo, Con-NF, or a custom OOD split) to demonstrate breadth.",
    "Neglecting the efficiency of the Lean verification loop: Lean-STaR explicitly notes that CPU/IO constraints in the Lean verification loop limited them to only 2 expert iteration rounds. Lean Workbook built a custom REPL for scalable compilation checking. Papers that use Lean as a verification oracle must engineer the verification pipeline for throughput—this is an infrastructure problem that can silently bottleneck otherwise good research. AlphaProof's compilation of Mathlib tactics to C (6× speedup) shows the payoff of investing in verification infrastructure."
  ],
  "field_trajectory": "The field is converging on three macro-directions that will define the next 2-3 years of research. First, the dissolution of the training-inference boundary: AlphaProof's Test-Time Reinforcement Learning demonstrated that systems can productively learn at inference time, generating problem-specific training curricula and running focused RL before producing answers. This paradigm will spread beyond olympiad-level provers to autoformalization itself—imagine an autoformalization system that, given a hard informal statement, generates a curriculum of related formalization problems, trains on them, and then tackles the target. The scaling laws here are barely understood, and the interaction between test-time compute and pre-training compute is an open theoretical question with enormous practical implications.\n\nSecond, autoformalization is becoming the central bottleneck rather than proving. AlphaProof showed that with 80M autoformalized problems, the RL prover achieves olympiad-level performance—but the autoformalization pipeline loses ~40% of hard problems. BEq showed that even evaluating autoformalization quality is unsolved. Lean Workbook showed that active learning can reach 93.5% on contest problems. The frontier is pushing autoformalization toward research-level mathematics, where problems are less formulaic, dependencies are deeper, and the gap between informal mathematical practice and formal library conventions is wider. Dependency-aware, retrieval-augmented autoformalization—where the model knows what exists in Mathlib before generating Lean code—will likely become the standard paradigm, replacing context-blind seq2seq translation.\n\nThird, the field is moving from individual-statement processing to theory-level formalization. Current systems translate one theorem at a time, but real mathematics consists of interconnected theories with shared definitions, notational conventions, and conceptual scaffolding. BEq's topological informalization of Mathlib's full dependency graph (24 generations, 243K objects) hints at what's possible. The next breakthrough may come from systems that formalize entire mathematical arguments—a paper's worth of definitions, lemmas, and theorems together—maintaining internal consistency and leveraging cross-theorem dependencies. This requires advances in long-context formal reasoning, hierarchical proof planning, and dependency management that go well beyond current capabilities.",
